<DocSection Title="What you'll do">
    <p>
        In this page you'll add a brand-new entity to the app you ran on the previous
        page — a <code>Warehouse</code> — and watch the framework wire it up across all
        four projects. The entity will get:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Two DTOs (a view DTO for the form and a list DTO) in <b>Shared</b></li>
        <li>An entity class, repository, and DbContext registration in <b>Data</b></li>
        <li>A REST/OData controller in <b>API</b></li>
        <li>List and form pages in <b>Web</b></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        All eight files are generated by a single command. After scaffolding,
        you'll add a few properties of your own, put them on the form and the list,
        create a migration, and see the new pages running in the browser.
    </p>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Note">
        Pick a name the sample app doesn't already use. The sample already has a
        <code>Vehicle</code> entity, for example, so <code>-n Vehicle</code> stops with
        <i>"Creating this template will make changes to existing files"</i> — and
        <code>--force</code> would overwrite the sample's own files.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Step 1: Scaffold the entity">
    <p>
        From inside your project's root directory (the folder that contains the
        <code>.sln</code> file), run:
    </p>

    <DocSnippet Name="ScaffoldEntity" Language="bash" />

    <p>Two parameters:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>-n Warehouse</code> — the name of your new entity. Use PascalCase, singular (<code>Warehouse</code>, not <code>Warehouses</code>).</li>
        <li><code>--solution MyFirstShiftApp</code> — the solution name you used when you ran <code>dotnet new shift</code>. The item template uses this to figure out which projects to drop files into.</li>
    </ul>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Note">
        The <code>shiftentity</code> item template was installed alongside the
        main <code>shift</code> template when you ran <code>dotnet new install ShiftSoftware.ShiftTemplates</code>
        on the Installation page. You don't need to install anything extra.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Step 2: See what got generated">
    <p>The command produces eight files across all four projects:</p>
    <pre><code>MyFirstShiftApp.Shared/DTOs/Warehouse/WarehouseDTO.cs        ← view DTO (used by the form, also for saving)
MyFirstShiftApp.Shared/DTOs/Warehouse/WarehouseListDTO.cs    ← list DTO

MyFirstShiftApp.Data/Entities/Warehouse.cs                   ← EF Core entity
MyFirstShiftApp.Data/DbContext/Warehouse.cs                  ← DbContext partial (adds the DbSet)
MyFirstShiftApp.Data/Repositories/WarehouseRepository.cs     ← repository

MyFirstShiftApp.API/Controllers/WarehouseController.cs       ← REST/OData controller

MyFirstShiftApp.Web/Pages/Warehouse/WarehouseList.razor      ← list page
MyFirstShiftApp.Web/Pages/Warehouse/WarehouseForm.razor      ← form page</code></pre>

    <p>
        The DbContext registration is the trick worth noticing.
        <code>MyFirstShiftApp.Data/DbContext/Warehouse.cs</code> is a
        <b>partial class</b> that adds <code>DbSet&lt;Warehouse&gt; Warehouse</code> to your
        existing <code>DB</code> context. You don't have to remember to register
        the entity manually — the partial class merges in automatically at compile time.
    </p>
    <p>
        There is no mapper file: ShiftMapper declares the maps between
        <code>Warehouse</code> and its two DTOs at build time, from the repository's type
        arguments. If a property ever needs more than a same-name copy, that customization
        goes in the project's one mapper class, <code>Mappers/MyFirstShiftAppMapper.cs</code>.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Step 3: Add some properties">
    <p>
        The generated entity is intentionally empty so you can shape it for your
        domain. Replace <code>MyFirstShiftApp.Data/Entities/Warehouse.cs</code> with:
    </p>
    <DocSnippet Name="WarehouseEntity" Language="cs" />

    <p>
        Then mirror those fields in the DTOs. The view DTO,
        <code>MyFirstShiftApp.Shared/DTOs/Warehouse/WarehouseDTO.cs</code>, carries everything
        the form shows and saves:
    </p>
    <DocSnippet Name="WarehouseDTO" Language="cs" />

    <p>The list DTO, <code>WarehouseListDTO.cs</code> next to it, carries only what the list shows:</p>
    <DocSnippet Name="WarehouseListDTO" Language="cs" />

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Tip">
        Keep <code>WarehouseListDTO</code> small — only the fields a user needs to see in
        a search results table. Put rich detail (large text, related collections,
        computed fields) on <code>WarehouseDTO</code> instead. The framework's OData
        pipeline projects directly to <code>WarehouseListDTO</code> for list endpoints,
        so anything you add there is sent on every page-of-results query.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

