        inputs just before the closing <code>&lt;/ShiftEntityForm&gt;</code>:
    </p>
    <DocSnippet Name="WarehouseFormFields" Language="razor" />

    <p>
        And in <code>WarehouseList.razor</code>, add the columns just before the closing
        <code>&lt;/ShiftList&gt;</code>:
    </p>
    <DocSnippet Name="WarehouseListColumns" Language="razor" />
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Step 5: Add a migration and apply it">
    <p>
        EF Core needs to know your schema changed. Add a migration capturing the
        new table:
    </p>
    <DocSnippet Name="AddWarehouseMigration" Language="bash" />

    <p>Then apply it to the database:</p>
    <DocSnippet Name="ApplyWarehouseMigration" Language="bash" />

    <p>You should see EF executing a CREATE TABLE statement for your new <code>Warehouse</code> table.</p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Step 6: Run the app and try it">
    <p>
        Start the API the same way you did on the previous page (<code>dotnet run --project MyFirstShiftApp.API</code>),
        sign in, then go to <code>/WarehouseList</code> in your browser (the new entity
        isn't in the side menu yet). You'll see an empty list with a + button. Click it,
        fill in a name, city and capacity, click <b>Create</b> — and your new row appears
        in the list.
    </p>

    <p>
        That's the entire rhythm. For every new entity in your app, you repeat the
        same steps: scaffold, customize the entity and DTOs, put the fields on the
        pages, add a migration, apply it — and the framework gives you a working
        list/form CRUD UI plus REST/OData endpoints.
    </p>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Warning">
        The generated controller and pages opt out of TypeAuth permission checks
        (<code>base(null)</code> in the controller, <code>TypeAuthAction="null"</code> on
        the pages) so the entity works immediately — which also means <b>any signed-in
        user can read and change it</b>. Before real use, add the entity to your app's
        <code>MyFirstShiftAppActionTree</code> (in <b>Shared/ActionTrees</b>) and pass that
        action to the controller and the pages, the way the sample's
        <code>ProductBrand</code> does.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

